🔍 FIND THE BUG — What's wrong with this code?

double balance = 0.1 + 0.2;
if (balance == 0.3) {
    System.out.println("Balance is 0.3");
} else {
    System.out.println("Balance is NOT 0.3 — it is: " + balance);
}

back.content:
Output (surprising!):
Balance is NOT 0.3 — it is: 0.30000000000000004

Bug: Floating-point precision error. 0.1 and 0.2 cannot be represented exactly in binary.

NEVER compare floating-point with == in Java!

✅ Fix 1 — Use threshold/epsilon:
if (Math.abs(balance - 0.3) < 0.0001) { ... }

✅ Fix 2 — Use BigDecimal for money:
import java.math.BigDecimal;
BigDecimal b = new BigDecimal("0.1").add(new BigDecimal("0.2"));
// b.compareTo(new BigDecimal("0.3")) == 0  → true!

💡 This is why banking software ALWAYS uses BigDecimal for financial calculations!

front.content:
What does the ternary operator do? Write the syntax.
Refactor this if-else to a ternary:

String category;
if (balance >= 100000) {
    category = "PREMIUM";
} else {
    category = "STANDARD";
}

back.content:
Syntax: condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse

Refactored:
String category = (balance >= 100000) ? "PREMIUM" : "STANDARD";

More examples:
int max = (a > b) ? a : b;                    // max of two numbers
String status = (txnAmt > 0) ? "CREDIT" : "DEBIT";  // banking domain

⚠️ Avoid nested ternaries — hard to read:
// BAD:
String tier = (bal>=500000) ? "GOLD" : (bal>=100000) ? "SILVER" : "BRONZE";
// GOOD: Use if-else for 3+ conditions

💡 Best practice: Use ternary ONLY for simple value assignment with exactly 2 outcomes.
Never use for side effects (method calls that do work other than return a value).

